---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';

const description =
  'Clipwell’s copyright approach: public-media limits, user responsibility, infringement reporting, repeat-infringer policy and links to the DMCA process.';
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Copyright Policy"
  description={description}
  path="/copyright-policy/"
>
  <p>
    Clipwell respects copyright and the people who create the media people want to save. The service
    is not a library and does not claim ownership of downloaded material. It is a short-lived
    technical tool that processes links supplied by users.
  </p>

  <h2>Public access is not permission</h2>

  <p>
    A public post can be protected by copyright, even when it is visible to everyone. Public
    visibility does not mean the work is in the public domain, licensed for reuse or free of
    restrictions. Clipwell does not grant permission to copy, edit, republish or monetise any video,
    audio, image, caption or other work.
  </p>

  <p>
    You may use the service for content you own or have permission to use, or where a valid legal
    exception applies in your jurisdiction. That determination is yours. If you are unsure, ask the
    rights holder or seek legal advice.
  </p>

  <h2>What the service does with media</h2>

  <p>
    When a user requests a download, Clipwell retrieves the public media from the source platform and
    writes it to temporary storage for that download. The file is served through a job-specific link
    and deleted automatically after a short period. Clipwell does not maintain a public catalogue,
    promote downloaded works or claim a licence over them.
  </p>

  <p>
    The service does not remove watermarks, DRM or attribution, and it does not access private
    content. Users remain responsible for what they submit and how they use the resulting file.
  </p>

  <h2>If your work appears on a platform</h2>

  <p>
    If you want a post removed from the platform itself, use the platform’s copyright reporting
    process first. Clipwell cannot remove content from Instagram, Facebook, YouTube, TikTok, Kick,
    Twitch or X, and cannot change what those services publish. A Clipwell report concerns a
    temporary download route or the service itself, not the original post.
  </p>

  <h2>Reporting copyright infringement to Clipwell</h2>

  <p>
    If you believe the service has been used to infringe your work, send a notice to
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.dmcaEmail}`}>{SITE.dmcaEmail}</a>. The
    <a href="/dmca-policy/">DMCA Policy</a> describes the required elements for a formal notice under
    the U.S. Digital Millennium Copyright Act. In general, include:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>your name and contact information;</li>
    <li>identification of the copyrighted work you own or are authorised to protect;</li>
    <li>the specific Clipwell URL or request information at issue, if available;</li>
    <li>a statement of your good-faith belief that the use is unauthorised;</li>
    <li>a statement, under penalty of perjury, that the information is accurate and that you are the owner or authorised agent; and</li>
    <li>your physical or electronic signature.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Because downloads are temporary, a job link may have expired before a notice is reviewed. Do not
    send passwords, cookies, private media or unrelated personal information. We may ask for
    clarification if a notice is incomplete.
  </p>

  <h2>Counter-notification</h2>

  <p>
    If material was removed or access was disabled because of a copyright notice and you believe the
    removal was a mistake or misidentification, you may send a counter-notification to
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.dmcaEmail}`}>{SITE.dmcaEmail}</a> with the elements described in the DMCA
    Policy. Do not use a counter-notice to avoid a legitimate claim; it carries legal consequences.
  </p>

  <h2>Repeat infringers</h2>

  <p>
    Clipwell may restrict or block users, IP addresses or requests associated with repeated
    infringement, abuse or attempts to bypass access controls. The service may also refuse requests at
    its discretion where continued processing would create a legal or operational risk.
  </p>

  <h2>Misrepresentation</h2>

  <p>
    Knowingly submitting a false infringement notice or counter-notice can create liability for
    damages, including costs and attorney’s fees. If you are unsure whether you own the rights or are
    authorised to act, consult a qualified lawyer before submitting a formal notice.
  </p>

  <h2>Licensing and permissions</h2>

  <p>
    For content you want to reuse, the cleanest route is usually direct permission from the creator
    or a clear licence. Save the licence terms with the file. Attribution may be required even when
    the licence permits commercial use.
  </p>

  <p>
    Copyright questions that are not formal notices can go to
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.legalEmail}`}>{SITE.legalEmail}</a>. This page explains the operator’s
    process and is not legal advice.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
